※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=10 diag=none

※ `continue` begins the loop's next turn, which is exactly what reaching the
※ end of the body does -- so it is lowered the same way: the step the iterator
※ takes where there is one, then the names the loop carries, then the memory.
※ A `while` has no step; its next turn is its condition again.

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    let total: mut u6 = 0u6
    foreach §counting i := 0u6…10u6:
        if i > 4u6:
            continue §counting
        total ← total + i
    ※ 0 + 1 + 2 + 3 + 4, the later turns going straight round again
    let stopped: mut u6 = 0u6
    while §waiting stopped < 10u6:
        stopped ← stopped + 1u6
        if stopped < 9u6:
            continue §waiting
    total + stopped - 10u6
